WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.417 --> 00:00:03.586
[ Music ]

00:00:15.598 --> 00:00:19.936
&gt;&gt; Since opening in 1985, "Les
Miserables" has become something

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.896
of an institution
here in the West End.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:25.275
Not only is it the world's
longest running musical it's

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:29.904
been seen by more than 60
million people in 42 countries

00:00:29.946 --> 00:00:32.782
and in 21 different languages.

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:35.452
&gt;&gt; If somebody had told
you that way back when,

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:36.578
would you have believed it?

00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:38.079
&gt;&gt; I'd have laughed.

00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:39.956
[laughter] Absolutely no way.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.249
&gt;&gt; You say you knew
you were working

00:00:41.291 --> 00:00:42.584
on something good though.

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.919
&gt;&gt; Yes. I absolutely -- I
loved it but there was no way

00:00:45.003 --> 00:00:48.006
that anyone can imagine
something running this long.

00:00:48.089 --> 00:00:51.092
It's crazy isn't it?
It's probably going to long --

00:00:51.134 --> 00:00:53.136
it will probably outlive me.
[laughter]

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:56.056
&gt;&gt; I think it's one of the
great stories of the triumph

00:00:56.097 --> 00:00:58.850
for the human spirit
and it's got --

00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:00.769
because it's got
so many characters,

00:01:00.852 --> 00:01:02.103
it's got so many ways in.

00:01:02.187 --> 00:01:05.940
You know, you've got
the story of Valjean,

00:01:05.982 --> 00:01:09.778
which advances the discovery
of virtue and the discovery

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:12.280
of faith, the discovery
of love for a child

00:01:12.363 --> 00:01:13.948
and what it's like
to be a parent.

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:17.285
What it's like to let a
child go out into the world.

00:01:17.368 --> 00:01:21.122
Then you've got the kind of more
teenage love triangle story.

00:01:21.164 --> 00:01:24.292
The story of unrequited
love with Eponine, kids who,

00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:29.297
you know, enjoyed Gavroche
and little Cosette's stories.

00:01:29.339 --> 00:01:32.884
And any obsessive
policemen can enjoy Javert.

00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:36.387
[music and glass breaking]

00:01:36.471 --> 00:01:40.642
&gt;&gt; Jean Valjean's crime was
stealing a loaf of bread.

00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:43.937
&gt;&gt; Help me please.

00:01:43.978 --> 00:01:46.439
&gt;&gt; It was the beginning of
a lifelong confrontation.

00:01:46.481 --> 00:01:49.109
&gt;&gt; "Les Mis" miraculously is
the longest running musical

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:53.905
in the world and it's -- I mean,
I'm as surprised as anybody.

00:01:53.988 --> 00:01:55.323
You know, I mean, I, you know,

00:01:55.365 --> 00:01:59.035
I always thought it was
a very terrific musical

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:02.038
but I never had any
thought that a show

00:02:02.122 --> 00:02:06.000
of such serious note
would actually run as long

00:02:06.084 --> 00:02:09.671
but it seems to touch
every generation everywhere

00:02:09.712 --> 00:02:10.839
in the world.

00:02:10.922 --> 00:02:13.091
I mean Victor Hugo's
story, you know,

00:02:13.174 --> 00:02:15.677
is about contemporary
people, even though it's set

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.053
in another period setting.

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:19.222
And every country in the
world seems to react to it.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:23.017
And of course Alain Boublil
and Claude-Michel's music,

00:02:23.059 --> 00:02:25.019
Claude-Michel Schonberg's music.

00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:28.940
I just -- is -- the songs
are absolutely fantastic

00:02:29.023 --> 00:02:31.860
and they're timeless
and it speaks

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:33.820
to an audience of any age.

00:02:33.862 --> 00:02:35.405
&gt;&gt; Let's not forget though

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:38.700
that Victor Hugo was already
an international celebrity

00:02:38.783 --> 00:02:41.035
by the time he got to age 30.

00:02:41.077 --> 00:02:43.121
He was known as the
prince of poets

00:02:43.204 --> 00:02:47.500
and was shall we say massive
within the Romantic Movement.

00:02:47.542 --> 00:02:50.336
More importantly, of course,
he was what you would;

00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:55.133
I suppose assume today, just
a huge celebrity end of.

00:02:55.216 --> 00:02:56.676
Who would have thought though

00:02:56.718 --> 00:03:00.513
that so many years later his
name would still be synonymous

00:03:00.555 --> 00:03:02.557
really with the world
of musical theater.

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:06.895
Not just really in his
home country or even indeed

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:09.564
in Britain but worldwide.

00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:11.191
&gt;&gt; Take him up.

00:03:11.232 --> 00:03:14.569
&gt;&gt; A dual of wills with the
fanatic police inspector Javert.

00:03:14.611 --> 00:03:16.446
&gt;&gt; Continue.

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:17.947
[bell chiming]

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:19.908
&gt;&gt; The hope of escape was
his only reason for living.

00:03:19.949 --> 00:03:22.452
&gt;&gt; Halt or I'll shoot.

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:24.245
[gunshot]

00:03:24.287 --> 00:03:25.413
&gt;&gt; It is the decision
of the military tribunal

00:03:25.496 --> 00:03:28.082
that five years be
added to your sentence.

00:03:28.166 --> 00:03:30.543
&gt;&gt; You must have been asked
many times but can you

00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:33.171
in any way put your
finger on what it is

00:03:33.254 --> 00:03:36.299
that has made it the
longest running show ever?

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:39.969
&gt;&gt; Well I personally
think it's the story.

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:42.680
&gt;&gt; Victor Hugo's
masterpiece "Les Miserables."

00:03:42.764 --> 00:03:44.140
&gt;&gt; You mustn't cry.

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:46.935
You must be very brave.

00:03:47.018 --> 00:03:48.561
&gt;&gt; With all the power
and spectacle

00:03:48.603 --> 00:03:51.356
of those turbulent years leading
to the French Revolution,

00:03:51.439 --> 00:03:56.319
it is a story of incredible
courage and relentless pursuit.

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:57.445
&gt;&gt; Join us.

00:03:57.487 --> 00:03:59.239
&gt;&gt; An exciting panorama
that captures the spirit

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.283
of a rebellious generation.

00:04:02.325 --> 00:04:04.827
&gt;&gt; It's the most
interesting universal story

00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:06.746
and it touches everybody.

00:04:06.788 --> 00:04:11.334
I remember watching some
of the scenes that I wasn't

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:16.089
in way back before it went on,
you know, during rehearsals

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.883
and I was sobbing my heart
out and then I had --

00:04:18.967 --> 00:04:21.135
I thought, oh my God, it's my
scene coming up next and I had

00:04:21.219 --> 00:04:22.804
to pull myself together.

00:04:22.845 --> 00:04:28.226
I mean this show -- well, it
makes men cry, put it that way.

00:04:28.309 --> 00:04:30.561
[laughter]

00:04:30.645 --> 00:04:32.480
&gt;&gt; It's a beautiful story.

00:04:32.563 --> 00:04:34.524
I mean whether you're
into musicals or not,

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:35.984
it's one of the greatest
stories ever told.

00:04:36.025 --> 00:04:45.576
[ Sounds of horses,
gunshots and crashing ]

00:04:50.832 --> 00:04:54.210
&gt;&gt; The crowning point really
for Victor Hugo's masterpiece

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:59.799
"Les Miserables" really
started way back in June 1832.

00:04:59.841 --> 00:05:02.176
He was sat in a park, relaxing,

00:05:02.218 --> 00:05:04.262
heard a rather strange
noise at first.

00:05:04.345 --> 00:05:07.348
Found out that it was
gunfire, which then led him

00:05:07.432 --> 00:05:11.269
in to the June revolution, which
of course then formed the basis

00:05:11.352 --> 00:05:15.857
of possibly his best
and most famous idea.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.234
&gt;&gt; You join us today.

00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:21.487
Together we will lay our hand on
the sword held of God and shout,

00:05:21.529 --> 00:05:25.033
down with the war bonds,
down with the Monarchy.

00:05:25.116 --> 00:05:26.743
&gt;&gt; It was the first
lesson I learned is

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:29.162
that it doesn't matter
how good the songs are.

00:05:29.203 --> 00:05:33.541
If the book isn't really
strong and the songs don't grow

00:05:33.583 --> 00:05:36.252
out of the book, you
don't have a good musical.

00:05:36.336 --> 00:05:40.381
&gt;&gt; Victor's first full length
novel was one that we know

00:05:40.423 --> 00:05:43.676
by a better title now, but when
it first came out, it was known

00:05:43.718 --> 00:05:47.347
as "Notre-Dame de Paris",
which then in turn became,

00:05:47.388 --> 00:05:50.350
of course more famous
as "The Hunchback

00:05:50.391 --> 00:05:54.479
of Notre Dame" being remade
so many times, most famously

00:05:54.562 --> 00:05:58.566
of course by Hollywood in the
late '30s with Charles Laughton.

00:05:58.608 --> 00:06:01.903
But that really did open
many doors for Victor

00:06:01.944 --> 00:06:03.613
and he became even
more celebrated

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:04.906
if that were possible.

00:06:04.947 --> 00:06:08.576
Now just to put this in sort
of a bracket if you like,

00:06:08.659 --> 00:06:12.246
Paris and France in particular
were already in the grip

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.624
of something rather
dramatic themselves.

00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:18.002
There had been a massive
outbreak of cholera

00:06:18.086 --> 00:06:20.963
where over 19,000
Parisians died.

00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:24.342
Now some people thought
that this was really set

00:06:24.425 --> 00:06:27.303
up by the government as
one of those mad theories,

00:06:27.387 --> 00:06:30.431
you know because they were
trying to poison off for want

00:06:30.473 --> 00:06:35.103
of a better word some of the
lesser desirable residents

00:06:35.186 --> 00:06:37.563
of Parisian Society,
the underdogs.

00:06:37.605 --> 00:06:40.566
And this of course then
fascinated Hugo himself giving

00:06:40.608 --> 00:06:45.279
him even more spark to an
already dramatic story.

00:06:45.363 --> 00:06:48.658
Now not many people will
know that Victor Hugo wrote

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:51.494
"Les Miserables" in
his long years of exile

00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:55.331
in the Channel Islands and
to get inspiration for this,

00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:59.877
he would write naked
at his desk every day.

00:06:59.961 --> 00:07:03.381
Shocking some people who would
actually turn up to take tea

00:07:03.464 --> 00:07:04.966
with him and things like that.

00:07:05.007 --> 00:07:06.467
I think what is interesting
to note

00:07:06.551 --> 00:07:10.513
as well he really didn't rush
this particular masterpiece.

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:12.974
He went through many,
many different rewrites

00:07:13.015 --> 00:07:15.977
and for a while it sat around.

00:07:16.018 --> 00:07:17.603
By himself, you know,
he changed plot.

00:07:17.645 --> 00:07:20.440
Decided to pick it up,
go back to it again.

00:07:20.481 --> 00:07:24.068
He was known as a perfectionist
and I think of course

00:07:24.152 --> 00:07:27.321
like anybody who'd enjoy
huge success it would be

00:07:27.363 --> 00:07:29.699
like today releasing
a Bond movie.

00:07:29.782 --> 00:07:31.826
You know, everybody's
going to criticize.

00:07:31.868 --> 00:07:34.662
Everybody's going to say whether
they like it or not and he was

00:07:34.704 --> 00:07:36.956
under immense pressure
to make sure what he came

00:07:36.998 --> 00:07:40.877
up with next was of course
an international smash

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:42.503
and of course when it first came

00:07:42.545 --> 00:07:45.506
out that wasn't necessarily
the case.

00:07:45.548 --> 00:07:49.802
Victor Hugo's "Les Miserables"
really was the most anticipated

00:07:49.844 --> 00:07:51.429
publication in years.

00:07:51.512 --> 00:07:54.182
And New York society
was a gag waiting

00:07:54.223 --> 00:07:55.683
to know how it would be done.

00:07:55.725 --> 00:07:59.228
And to give you just an
insight really as to how

00:07:59.312 --> 00:08:01.689
that even back then
they were very clever.

00:08:01.731 --> 00:08:05.109
They decided to mount
this massive PR campaign.

00:08:05.193 --> 00:08:08.529
And so they would drop in little
teasers rather like what they do

00:08:08.613 --> 00:08:11.574
with say like the "Harry
Potter" movie in today's decade.

00:08:11.657 --> 00:08:14.494
So they just drop a
little bit in and they came

00:08:14.535 --> 00:08:17.872
up with the first sort of take,
the second take and by the time

00:08:17.914 --> 00:08:21.375
of course that the final volume
was released it was a momentum.

00:08:21.417 --> 00:08:23.252
Everybody wanted a copy.

00:08:23.336 --> 00:08:25.630
Everybody needed to be seen
with a copy of their book.

00:08:25.713 --> 00:08:27.757
It was the coffee
table equivalent

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.175
of gold dust if you like.

00:08:29.217 --> 00:08:32.887
And once again, of course, he
was looking at a mega smash hit.

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:35.515
&gt;&gt; Cameron Mackintosh was
sent a demo tape based

00:08:35.556 --> 00:08:37.433
on Victor Hugo's novel
"Les Miserables."

00:08:37.517 --> 00:08:40.645
The two French composers
Claude-Michel Schonberg

00:08:40.728 --> 00:08:45.358
and Alain Boublil worked closely
with him developing the score.

00:08:45.399 --> 00:08:47.902
&gt;&gt; We never go ahead with
anything unless all three

00:08:47.944 --> 00:08:52.532
of us absolutely agree
on every step of the way.

00:08:52.573 --> 00:08:55.826
&gt;&gt; I think that whole
da, da, da --

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:58.037
&gt;&gt; I can't do without
the first part.

00:08:58.079 --> 00:09:00.748
&gt;&gt; Because you can't do
da, da, da, da, da, de, de.

00:09:00.831 --> 00:09:04.752
&gt;&gt; It's like a safety
break that if two --

00:09:04.835 --> 00:09:07.088
if two like something
and the other doesn't,

00:09:07.129 --> 00:09:10.550
it means that actually we
haven't got the right solution

00:09:10.591 --> 00:09:13.302
and we then carry on until
we find a third solution.

00:09:13.386 --> 00:09:16.597
&gt;&gt; When Victor Hugo
passed away in May 1885,

00:09:16.639 --> 00:09:19.600
he was considered
a national hero.

00:09:19.642 --> 00:09:22.228
Over 2 million people
mourned at his funeral,

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:23.980
but this is the interesting
thing.

00:09:24.063 --> 00:09:26.607
The theme of course throughout
"Les Miserables" is all

00:09:26.691 --> 00:09:29.610
about social injustice
and he'd requested

00:09:29.652 --> 00:09:31.904
that he wanted a
pauper's funeral.

00:09:31.946 --> 00:09:35.199
Suddenly though for him,
that wish was not carried out

00:09:35.283 --> 00:09:40.288
and he became, if possible, an
even bigger cultural literally

00:09:40.371 --> 00:09:42.373
and of course celebrity icon.

00:09:42.456 --> 00:09:44.458
&gt;&gt; When I first heard it
was called "Les Miserables",

00:09:44.542 --> 00:09:47.253
I thought hum, I
thought, I don't know

00:09:47.295 --> 00:09:49.171
if that name's going to work.

00:09:49.255 --> 00:09:50.590
And so we called it "The Glums."

00:09:50.631 --> 00:09:51.716
And now,

00:09:51.799 --> 00:09:54.176
you know, "Les Miserables"
is the name

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.762
that everybody knows
worldwide and even

00:09:56.804 --> 00:09:58.806
if you don't speak
French you know the name.

00:09:58.848 --> 00:10:02.143
&gt;&gt; Since the publication of "Les
Miserables" there's been no less

00:10:02.226 --> 00:10:07.481
than 48 operatic versions, but
the version really that we know

00:10:07.565 --> 00:10:12.069
and love today actually
started out way back in 1980

00:10:12.153 --> 00:10:14.655
as just a simple concept album.

00:10:14.697 --> 00:10:16.949
&gt;&gt; Can you not actually get
that with a different --

00:10:16.991 --> 00:10:19.035
slightly different rhythm
because that's got --

00:10:19.118 --> 00:10:21.912
that's got the menace
that we're talking about.

00:10:21.996 --> 00:10:24.665
&gt;&gt; I think a producer
shouldn't write for writers.

00:10:24.749 --> 00:10:26.626
I don't think directors
should do that.

00:10:26.667 --> 00:10:28.544
&gt;&gt; The writers and lyricists

00:10:28.628 --> 00:10:33.007
of the wonderful concept
album were allegedly inspired

00:10:33.049 --> 00:10:37.595
by another British phenomenon
that of Lionel Bart's "Oliver",

00:10:37.678 --> 00:10:39.597
the record breaking musical.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:42.016
Apparently they were sat
down watching it in a theater

00:10:42.058 --> 00:10:46.103
and particularly taking
interest in the artful dodger

00:10:46.187 --> 00:10:49.523
and as they were watching
Oliver unfold on stage,

00:10:49.565 --> 00:10:51.692
they decided how
they could almost see

00:10:51.776 --> 00:10:53.861
"Les Miserables" taking shape

00:10:53.944 --> 00:10:57.615
in their own musical
opera themselves simply

00:10:57.698 --> 00:10:59.408
by just watching "Oliver."

00:10:59.492 --> 00:11:02.119
Now who would have thought
that a simple project

00:11:02.203 --> 00:11:03.579
like Lionel Bart's "Oliver"

00:11:03.663 --> 00:11:05.790
which also had its
own problems just like

00:11:05.873 --> 00:11:09.752
"Les Miserables" would explode
from just an afternoon matinee.

00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:13.798
&gt;&gt; We are there to compliment
the writer's imagination

00:11:13.881 --> 00:11:19.261
and to bring -- bring that
writing to its best fruition.

00:11:19.345 --> 00:11:22.723
What I'm very good at is
saying this bit's dull.

00:11:22.807 --> 00:11:24.725
This song does not sort
of come to a proper climax

00:11:24.767 --> 00:11:35.736
and I'm just fascinated about
how -- how the writing develops

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:39.573
and how that you take
a basically good story

00:11:39.615 --> 00:11:42.618
and you make sure that the graph

00:11:42.702 --> 00:11:45.788
of the motion throughout the
evening is hit exactly right.

00:11:45.871 --> 00:11:48.582
&gt;&gt; Three months after the
concert album had topped the

00:11:48.624 --> 00:11:51.252
charts in France,
someone decided

00:11:51.293 --> 00:11:56.716
to take the rather risky venture
of taking it to the stage.

00:11:56.757 --> 00:11:59.468
Once again the critics
were less than nice

00:11:59.552 --> 00:12:02.888
about this particular
new musical but word

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:06.892
of mouth took off once again
making the three-month run

00:12:06.934 --> 00:12:08.269
a sellout.

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:11.397
Of course by this point, other
people were taking notice,

00:12:11.439 --> 00:12:14.483
not just in France,
but around the world.

00:12:14.567 --> 00:12:15.985
&gt;&gt; That's the thing
that I always aim

00:12:16.068 --> 00:12:17.695
for for my productions.

00:12:17.778 --> 00:12:20.531
Once the audience settles down,
the first thing you've got

00:12:20.614 --> 00:12:22.491
to do is in the first
five minutes is

00:12:22.575 --> 00:12:24.952
to tell them the
language of the evening

00:12:24.994 --> 00:12:26.245
that you're going to use.

00:12:26.287 --> 00:12:31.292
&gt;&gt; Way back in 1980, the
first original concept album

00:12:31.375 --> 00:12:33.836
of "Les Mis" was released.

00:12:33.919 --> 00:12:36.714
It topped the charts without
really much promotion.

00:12:36.797 --> 00:12:38.966
And three months
later they decided

00:12:39.049 --> 00:12:43.387
to stage the very first
musical version based

00:12:43.471 --> 00:12:44.930
on the concept album.

00:12:44.972 --> 00:12:47.892
This just broke box
office records

00:12:47.975 --> 00:12:51.061
with over a million plus people
wanting to see the production.

00:12:51.145 --> 00:12:53.689
Then, of course, this was
brought to the attention

00:12:53.773 --> 00:12:56.776
of who we know now to
be the super producer,

00:12:56.817 --> 00:13:00.112
none other than our very
own Cameron Mackintosh

00:13:00.154 --> 00:13:04.283
but it would take him quite some
time before he could turn it

00:13:04.325 --> 00:13:07.369
into the British phenomenon
that we know and love

00:13:07.453 --> 00:13:08.954
and take it around the world.

00:13:08.996 --> 00:13:11.707
But when he first heard
the concept album,

00:13:11.791 --> 00:13:13.793
he thought there could
be something in it.

00:13:13.834 --> 00:13:15.878
Of course he had no idea just

00:13:15.961 --> 00:13:19.757
that something would make
him very, very rich indeed.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:23.302
&gt;&gt; Okay. One, two, three, whoa.

00:13:23.344 --> 00:13:25.888
&gt;&gt; I knew from the beginning I
wanted to do the singing live

00:13:25.971 --> 00:13:28.516
and I was so passionate
about it I wasn't willing

00:13:28.599 --> 00:13:32.102
to make the film unless I could
do the singing live and this is

00:13:32.186 --> 00:13:35.815
because I always find
singing to play back,

00:13:35.856 --> 00:13:38.651
to lip sync to prerecorded
however it is done

00:13:38.692 --> 00:13:39.860
to be an artifice.

00:13:39.902 --> 00:13:41.612
At its worse, it
makes you embarrassed.

00:13:41.695 --> 00:13:44.865
The best just forgive
it and in the light

00:13:44.907 --> 00:13:47.910
of a comedic film it's
easier to forgive.

00:13:47.993 --> 00:13:51.956
I wanted there to be no
barrier between the audience

00:13:52.039 --> 00:13:54.124
and the experience
and those emotions.

00:13:54.208 --> 00:14:00.297
[ Background noise ]

00:14:00.381 --> 00:14:03.884
In terms of how it was made it
was a truly British production.

00:14:03.926 --> 00:14:07.638
Internationalism was, you
know, really in the cards

00:14:07.721 --> 00:14:09.014
because I wanted some
of the best people

00:14:09.056 --> 00:14:10.724
in the world to do this.

00:14:10.766 --> 00:14:13.394
That doesn't mean the British
are the best singers but,

00:14:13.435 --> 00:14:14.770
you know, [inaudible]

00:14:14.854 --> 00:14:17.398
&gt;&gt; I think what's personally
interesting to take note

00:14:17.439 --> 00:14:22.069
of is the fact that when the
show launched in October 1985,

00:14:22.111 --> 00:14:24.113
nobody really could have
foreseen just how big it was

00:14:24.196 --> 00:14:25.239
going to be.

00:14:25.281 --> 00:14:28.117
And you know the critics
as I say loathed it.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:29.285
Rather cruel in fact.

00:14:29.368 --> 00:14:32.830
Not really just about the
length of the musical,

00:14:32.913 --> 00:14:34.957
you know the fact that
we're getting a free ticket

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.251
and the fact that they
had to sit and endure it

00:14:37.293 --> 00:14:39.795
but they didn't really
understand even when it started

00:14:39.879 --> 00:14:42.756
to take off and become a
huge box office phenomena.

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.050
They couldn't understand
why the public liked it.

00:14:45.092 --> 00:14:50.639
&gt;&gt; The posh papers said
that we had trivialized one

00:14:50.723 --> 00:14:52.558
of the greatest works
of western literature.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:57.104
The tabloids agreed
between themselves

00:14:57.146 --> 00:15:03.903
that we had perpetrated a
gloomy, dull, endless evening,

00:15:03.944 --> 00:15:05.946
which wasn't what
musicals were about at all.

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:09.325
Musicals should be light and
frothy and frivolous evenings

00:15:09.408 --> 00:15:12.286
and sent everybody away
happy and humming the tunes.

00:15:12.369 --> 00:15:15.372
&gt;&gt; It took two years for
Cameron Mackintosh and his team

00:15:15.456 --> 00:15:18.250
to make the English language
version of what we know

00:15:18.292 --> 00:15:20.044
as "Les Miserables" today.

00:15:20.127 --> 00:15:24.298
But in October 1985,
it opened at the less

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:28.802
than glamorous location off
West End in the Barbican.

00:15:30.888 --> 00:15:32.681
&gt;&gt; Although his father
is Scottish,

00:15:32.765 --> 00:15:35.225
Mackintosh himself was brought
up in the Home Counties.

00:15:35.309 --> 00:15:37.728
His mother is Maltese
and his parents met

00:15:37.811 --> 00:15:40.230
in ANSA during the war.

00:15:40.314 --> 00:15:45.819
&gt;&gt; When he was young, he was
always standing on chairs,

00:15:45.861 --> 00:15:47.821
with a stick in his hand

00:15:47.905 --> 00:15:50.908
as if he had a great
orchestra in front.

00:15:50.991 --> 00:15:56.372
And I thought he was going to be
sort of a bandmaster, bandleader

00:15:56.455 --> 00:15:57.831
or something like that.

00:15:57.873 --> 00:16:01.627
Then he started with
a puppet show.

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:06.131
&gt;&gt; I know a lot of
people do puppet shows

00:16:06.173 --> 00:16:10.177
but they don't normally drive
the family entirely batty

00:16:10.219 --> 00:16:11.261
all year.

00:16:13.681 --> 00:16:15.015
&gt;&gt; The first thing
he used to tell us,

00:16:15.057 --> 00:16:17.977
"If you're late,
you won't come in."

00:16:18.018 --> 00:16:20.854
So we used to say,
"Well, we don't come

00:16:20.896 --> 00:16:21.897
in you'll have no audience."

00:16:21.981 --> 00:16:22.982
He'd said, "Never mind.

00:16:23.023 --> 00:16:25.317
Every show you must be on time."

00:16:25.359 --> 00:16:29.279
&gt;&gt; I just finished
playing a performance,

00:16:29.363 --> 00:16:32.658
imaginary performance
of sadder days

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:38.914
and the stage door keeper came
to my dressing room and said,

00:16:38.998 --> 00:16:43.335
"Excuse me sir, I'm sorry to
bother you, but there's a lady

00:16:43.377 --> 00:16:47.881
at the stage door with a
little boy and she says

00:16:47.965 --> 00:16:51.677
that he absolutely
insists upon seeing you.

00:16:51.719 --> 00:16:56.890
So in you came, Cameron,
and was it your aunt

00:16:56.932 --> 00:16:58.392
that was with you, I think?

00:16:58.475 --> 00:17:00.853
&gt;&gt; It was my aunt, my
mother and my kilt.

00:17:00.894 --> 00:17:01.937
&gt;&gt; [laughter] Yes.

00:17:02.021 --> 00:17:04.481
I remember your kilt very well.

00:17:04.565 --> 00:17:07.317
&gt;&gt; And Julian instead of sort
of patting me on the head

00:17:07.401 --> 00:17:10.070
and giving me some candy floss
actually took me terribly

00:17:10.154 --> 00:17:14.992
seriously and showed me
backstage of how the --

00:17:15.075 --> 00:17:17.911
he mimed with mini the
magic piano from the pit

00:17:17.953 --> 00:17:19.538
and how the flying
saucer worked.

00:17:19.580 --> 00:17:21.248
It was attached to wires.

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:23.375
And I just remember sitting
on the stage or standing

00:17:23.417 --> 00:17:24.918
on the stage looking
around and thinking yes,

00:17:25.002 --> 00:17:26.462
I'd like to do this
when I grow up.

00:17:26.545 --> 00:17:29.423
My nickname at school
was Darryl F. Mackintosh.

00:17:29.465 --> 00:17:32.801
After Darryl F. Zanuck
who was having lots

00:17:32.885 --> 00:17:34.094
of successful films then.

00:17:34.178 --> 00:17:37.389
And again, I used to
start the first day

00:17:37.431 --> 00:17:40.100
of the autumn term preparing
for the Christmas review.

00:17:40.184 --> 00:17:43.312
If people booked in advance
they could get the tickets

00:17:43.395 --> 00:17:44.813
for a shilling.

00:17:44.897 --> 00:17:46.523
And if they got [laughter]
nearer the date,

00:17:46.607 --> 00:17:48.901
they had to pay two shillings.

00:17:48.942 --> 00:17:50.277
I was like a Hoover.

00:17:50.319 --> 00:17:54.406
I sucked up any information
about the theater.

00:17:54.448 --> 00:17:55.991
I got magazines from America.

00:17:56.075 --> 00:17:59.787
From every newspaper
article I could

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:01.371
about anything about
the theater.

00:18:01.455 --> 00:18:04.875
I was a walking encyclopedia at
school and drove everybody mad

00:18:04.958 --> 00:18:07.544
who was much more interested
the football results.

00:18:07.628 --> 00:18:09.671
&gt;&gt; When he left school
Cameron Mackintosh decided

00:18:09.755 --> 00:18:11.173
to try the stage
management course

00:18:11.256 --> 00:18:14.176
at the Central School
of Speech and Drama.

00:18:14.259 --> 00:18:17.513
But he left after only a year to
get proper backstage experience

00:18:17.596 --> 00:18:19.556
at the Theatre Royal Drury Lane.

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:24.019
In the mornings he also cleaned
the theatre to earn extra money.

00:18:24.103 --> 00:18:26.730
&gt;&gt; I took great pride
cleaning that theatre.

00:18:26.814 --> 00:18:28.982
The dresser at the
bar was my domain

00:18:29.066 --> 00:18:31.735
and I'd very carefully
polish all the brass

00:18:31.819 --> 00:18:33.153
and wash the glass.

00:18:33.195 --> 00:18:34.988
&gt;&gt; So it must be rather
nice for you to be back

00:18:35.072 --> 00:18:36.865
at Drury Lane in "Miss Saigon."

00:18:36.949 --> 00:18:39.535
&gt;&gt; It's -- oh it's
like coming home.

00:18:39.618 --> 00:18:42.121
I've always had -- that's
the one dream I've had is

00:18:42.162 --> 00:18:43.747
to have a show on at Drury Lane.

00:18:43.831 --> 00:18:47.292
&gt;&gt; He did have a variety of
jobs backstage including a spell

00:18:47.334 --> 00:18:50.420
up in the flies hauling
scenery up and down.

00:18:50.504 --> 00:18:55.259
&gt;&gt; As I undid the ropes,
they caught around my hand

00:18:55.342 --> 00:18:59.012
and I was flown the
weight of this huge piece

00:18:59.096 --> 00:19:01.431
of scenery took me
right up to the grid

00:19:01.515 --> 00:19:05.144
and it stopped literally
six inches from the grid.

00:19:05.185 --> 00:19:08.230
I would have -- only because it
was a very tall piece of scenery

00:19:08.313 --> 00:19:10.566
that had landed on the stage
and they had to cut me down.

00:19:10.649 --> 00:19:11.859
[laughter]

00:19:11.942 --> 00:19:14.361
&gt;&gt; You know people
think it's pretty easy

00:19:14.403 --> 00:19:15.028
to be an [inaudible].

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:18.031
Believe me it ain't.

00:19:18.073 --> 00:19:19.366
It ain't easy at all.

00:19:19.408 --> 00:19:22.202
&gt;&gt; Choreographers and
directors always argue.

00:19:22.286 --> 00:19:25.664
The book writer tends to argue
with the composer and lyricist

00:19:25.706 --> 00:19:28.208
and it's very important to have
a strong producer at the top

00:19:28.250 --> 00:19:30.669
because in a sense
he is the one who has

00:19:30.711 --> 00:19:34.256
to balance all the elements
and control all the elements.

00:19:34.339 --> 00:19:39.887
&gt;&gt; I knew the moment I started
to talk to Cameron in detail

00:19:39.970 --> 00:19:42.472
that there was a man
after my own heart.

00:19:42.556 --> 00:19:44.224
He had just the same enthusiasm.

00:19:44.308 --> 00:19:47.769
Just the same care that
he must be glamorous

00:19:47.853 --> 00:19:49.062
and beautifully done.

00:19:49.146 --> 00:19:51.481
That you don't save
pennies on the costume

00:19:51.565 --> 00:19:54.735
or save pennies on the
scenery or something.

00:19:54.776 --> 00:19:56.069
You do it properly.

00:19:56.153 --> 00:19:58.572
You don't try and scrape
into a smaller orchestra.

00:19:58.614 --> 00:20:01.074
&gt;&gt; I'm not a great
musician though I can tell

00:20:01.116 --> 00:20:03.410
when the music's right or wrong.

00:20:03.452 --> 00:20:08.582
I can overlap into his area
by sort of prodding him here

00:20:08.624 --> 00:20:11.460
and there and thinking don't we
need something like that or is

00:20:11.543 --> 00:20:15.297
that mood right or
whatever in his scoring.

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:19.092
&gt;&gt; Cameron is really much more
than old-fashioned producer

00:20:19.176 --> 00:20:22.179
in the sense that he does
have strong creative views

00:20:22.262 --> 00:20:24.139
and sometimes [laughter]
the team he works

00:20:24.223 --> 00:20:27.267
with don't share them but
he has a very strong opinion

00:20:27.309 --> 00:20:28.936
practically about everything.

00:20:29.019 --> 00:20:31.355
In fact one of his qualities is
that you could be two minutes

00:20:31.438 --> 00:20:34.858
into a song and he'll come up
and say, "I don't like that.

00:20:34.942 --> 00:20:36.151
It doesn't work."

00:20:36.235 --> 00:20:37.819
And then you go, "Cameron
just wait a minute.

00:20:37.903 --> 00:20:39.947
Let's just get through to the
end and we'll work it out."

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:42.115
&gt;&gt; Well I can't help myself.

00:20:42.199 --> 00:20:43.617
I mean, I'm one of these people

00:20:43.659 --> 00:20:47.287
that just says what I feel
even if it isn't right.

00:20:47.371 --> 00:20:50.207
I just have to get it out of my
system but I've got a sort of --

00:20:50.290 --> 00:20:56.213
I sort of like sort of truffle
hound sort of smelling spots

00:20:56.296 --> 00:20:58.632
in the show which aren't --
that I don't think are right

00:20:58.715 --> 00:21:01.927
and I just keep on and on and
on until they just to shut me

00:21:01.969 --> 00:21:03.720
up they'll go away or
at least think about it.

00:21:03.804 --> 00:21:08.308
&gt;&gt; He had one complete
set of orchestrations made

00:21:08.392 --> 00:21:10.310
and then he decided that
he didn't like them.

00:21:10.394 --> 00:21:12.437
It wasn't right for the show.

00:21:12.479 --> 00:21:16.275
He got rid of that orchestrator
and hired another orchestrator

00:21:16.316 --> 00:21:19.278
who then did two more dummy
runs of orchestrations.

00:21:19.319 --> 00:21:23.198
&gt;&gt; I had to scrap an
entire concept album,

00:21:23.282 --> 00:21:29.121
which cost over 300,000-pounds,
which went down the Suwannee

00:21:29.162 --> 00:21:32.332
but it was worth it in the end.

00:21:32.374 --> 00:21:36.003
&gt;&gt; Have you ever seen a zebra
or giraffe in the jungle?

00:21:36.044 --> 00:21:37.337
I mean they -- [laughter]

00:21:37.379 --> 00:21:41.008
But I have last year
and I can tell you --

00:21:41.091 --> 00:21:43.010
&gt;&gt; To a certain extent
now we live

00:21:43.051 --> 00:21:44.761
in a world that's
divided into two.

00:21:44.845 --> 00:21:47.848
Stephen Sondheim gets all the
critical acclaim and the shows

00:21:47.889 --> 00:21:49.391
that Cameron Mackintosh

00:21:49.474 --> 00:21:50.976
and Andrew Lloyd Webber do are
derived as tourist schlock.

00:21:51.018 --> 00:21:56.898
Producers reign as kings of the
theatre coincide for the amount

00:21:56.982 --> 00:21:59.234
of time that the public's taste.

00:21:59.318 --> 00:22:01.528
Also happens to be the
producer's tastes and when

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:04.781
that changes then no matter
how successful you are,

00:22:04.865 --> 00:22:06.742
you go out the window.

00:22:06.825 --> 00:22:08.327
This has happened
to David Merrick.

00:22:08.368 --> 00:22:10.287
This has happened
to Harold Fielding,

00:22:10.370 --> 00:22:13.623
which one day will happen
to Cameron Mackintosh too.

00:22:13.707 --> 00:22:16.543
&gt;&gt; One thing when I started
off I was given a book

00:22:16.626 --> 00:22:19.046
about Cochran called
Shaman Looks On.

00:22:19.087 --> 00:22:20.339
It's an autobiography.

00:22:20.380 --> 00:22:23.008
And one thing stuck
early on in my life.

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:25.427
It was his advice to
aspiring young producers.

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:27.721
He said never put a show
on for the audiences.

00:22:27.804 --> 00:22:30.307
Always put it on for yourself
and do it as best that you can

00:22:30.390 --> 00:22:33.894
and then maybe an audience
will come and see it.

00:22:33.935 --> 00:22:35.854
And I think that's
pretty good advice.

00:22:35.896 --> 00:22:39.274
&gt;&gt; It's not just a West End
phenomenon that continues

00:22:39.358 --> 00:22:42.319
to be a box office
hit around the globe;

00:22:42.402 --> 00:22:46.698
a staggering 56 million people
have now seen "Les Mis".

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:49.159
That's almost the equivalent
of every man, woman,

00:22:49.242 --> 00:22:52.996
and child living
in this country.

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:56.375
&gt;&gt; Michael Ball sings with Lea
Salonga, but this is not a scene

00:22:56.416 --> 00:22:59.294
from the hit show's "Phantom
of the Opera" or "Miss Saigon."

00:22:59.378 --> 00:23:02.672
It's another hit both have shown
in separately "Les Miserables."

00:23:02.756 --> 00:23:05.258
But everyone here's being
very jolly because it's

00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:07.010
"Les Mis'" 10th birthday.

00:23:07.094 --> 00:23:09.429
And hundreds of the cast
from its productions

00:23:09.513 --> 00:23:12.307
around the world are giving
a gala concert on Sunday

00:23:12.391 --> 00:23:14.434
but without props or costumes

00:23:14.518 --> 00:23:17.020
and new cast members it
can get confusing which way

00:23:17.104 --> 00:23:21.400
to go off stage but everything
should be smoothed out by Sunday

00:23:21.441 --> 00:23:24.277
when thousands of adoring fans
descend on the Albert Hall.

00:23:24.361 --> 00:23:27.739
&gt;&gt; The seal of making this
successful was the fact

00:23:27.781 --> 00:23:33.495
that it won best musical at the
Lawrence Olivier Awards in 1985.

00:23:33.578 --> 00:23:36.706
For them that suddenly
really had a bit of a clout

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:39.876
and suddenly people
decided to take notice.

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:44.297
They shot out of the Barbican
and into the West End,

00:23:44.381 --> 00:23:47.926
where it has basically
remained for the last 25 years.

00:23:47.968 --> 00:23:51.596
And when you think about it,
you know, it just goes to show

00:23:51.638 --> 00:23:53.974
that even though, you know,
people would say oh no,

00:23:54.015 --> 00:23:55.642
you know, the theme
is not really good

00:23:55.725 --> 00:23:57.894
and it's not a warm
sort of topic.

00:23:57.978 --> 00:23:59.354
Of course it's not.

00:23:59.438 --> 00:24:01.982
Victor Hugo wanted to get
social injustice out there

00:24:02.065 --> 00:24:03.483
and that's what he did.

00:24:03.567 --> 00:24:06.403
But the songs are so memorable
and have been recorded

00:24:06.486 --> 00:24:10.782
from everyone from the likes
of Dame Shirley Bassey right

00:24:10.824 --> 00:24:13.577
through to some of our
most celebrated opera stars

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:16.746
including, of course
Luciano Pavarotti,

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:20.041
but I think what really
cemented it in the end was

00:24:20.125 --> 00:24:22.461
that the public pushed
this through.

00:24:22.502 --> 00:24:25.255
And as someone who attends
many sorts of opening nights

00:24:25.338 --> 00:24:28.008
in the theaters, "Les
Mis" fans are nothing

00:24:28.091 --> 00:24:29.968
like I've ever met before.

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:31.470
They're very loyal.

00:24:31.511 --> 00:24:34.848
Some people, in fact, actually
book the same seats every

00:24:34.931 --> 00:24:38.310
matinee and turn up week
after week after week.

00:24:38.351 --> 00:24:40.145
They know the show backwards.

00:24:40.187 --> 00:24:41.688
They know everything
about you know

00:24:41.771 --> 00:24:44.524
when the lights should
come on, the scene changes,

00:24:44.566 --> 00:24:46.151
if somebody's feeling
ill in the cast.

00:24:46.193 --> 00:24:48.862
They become like friends and
one of the connecting factors

00:24:48.904 --> 00:24:52.365
that I think Victor Hugo
would really love is the fact

00:24:52.449 --> 00:24:55.410
that of course the cast then
connects with their fans,

00:24:55.494 --> 00:24:57.954
their audience and then
actually brings some sort

00:24:58.038 --> 00:24:59.414
of social connection.

00:24:59.498 --> 00:25:02.542
I'd like to think it's something
he really would admire.

00:25:02.584 --> 00:25:04.794
&gt;&gt; Well it started at the
Royal Shakespeare Company

00:25:04.878 --> 00:25:09.341
and the first performance was
five hours long and so they had

00:25:09.382 --> 00:25:12.469
to condense this amazing
story into three hours

00:25:12.552 --> 00:25:15.972
and in fact they cut out
a lot of fantastic things.

00:25:16.056 --> 00:25:19.726
There should be another
show, "Les Mis" the out cuts

00:25:19.809 --> 00:25:21.895
or whatever you call
it [laughter]

00:25:21.978 --> 00:25:25.649
So Royal Shakespeare Company,
we did it there for eight weeks

00:25:25.732 --> 00:25:28.360
and we had no idea it was going
to transfer to the West End.

00:25:28.401 --> 00:25:31.112
&gt;&gt; It's back to its
roots; the Barbican Theatre

00:25:31.196 --> 00:25:35.742
where it started two and a half
decades ago to some bad reviews

00:25:35.825 --> 00:25:38.578
and was just about to
go to the West End.

00:25:38.620 --> 00:25:41.540
&gt;&gt; Of course we were all a
bit depressed having seen

00:25:41.581 --> 00:25:44.626
such a fantastic
reception the night before

00:25:44.709 --> 00:25:48.088
and so I thought I'll get rid
of all the bad news at once

00:25:48.129 --> 00:25:50.590
and ring through the box office
and it took me several goes

00:25:50.632 --> 00:25:52.509
and that was where
the story came from.

00:25:52.592 --> 00:25:54.886
When they said how did you
get through, we've taken --

00:25:54.928 --> 00:25:57.931
we've had 5000 calls it's
a record at the Barbican.

00:25:57.973 --> 00:26:00.183
&gt;&gt; I knew it was going
to be a big hit years ago

00:26:00.267 --> 00:26:02.394
but I didn't know it was
going to last 21 years.

00:26:02.435 --> 00:26:03.311
It's fantastic.

00:26:03.395 --> 00:26:05.188
&gt;&gt; It's overwhelming.

00:26:05.272 --> 00:26:08.275
Absolutely overwhelming and I
don't necessary know that any

00:26:08.358 --> 00:26:11.611
of us truly understand
the significance of it

00:26:11.695 --> 00:26:15.282
and ultimately and
hopefully the impact.

00:26:15.323 --> 00:26:18.577
&gt;&gt; It's actually
quite fantastic though

00:26:18.618 --> 00:26:22.163
because the piece is really
about age and youth and to think

00:26:22.247 --> 00:26:26.334
that I did it 21 years ago
when I was the youngest adult

00:26:26.418 --> 00:26:29.045
and now people are
playing my role

00:26:29.129 --> 00:26:30.922
that weren't even
born back then.

00:26:30.964 --> 00:26:33.800
&gt;&gt; But it is really
interesting because of Nick --

00:26:33.842 --> 00:26:34.801
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:26:34.843 --> 00:26:36.761
&gt;&gt; -- to see so many
young people

00:26:36.803 --> 00:26:39.639
who probably have never
a musical ever, you know,

00:26:39.723 --> 00:26:41.391
coming in tonight being
completely enthralled.

00:26:41.474 --> 00:26:45.604
I mean, you could hear a
pin drop through the story.

00:26:45.645 --> 00:26:48.815
You know? And they just
reacted to the show and the fact

00:26:48.898 --> 00:26:51.735
that he was incredibly good
in it as was Camilla you know

00:26:51.818 --> 00:26:56.239
in their baptism fire tonight
but I was really very happy.

00:26:56.323 --> 00:26:57.574
&gt;&gt; It is nice being
back on stage.

00:26:57.657 --> 00:27:01.077
I was Gavroche when I was
in it last back in 2003

00:27:01.161 --> 00:27:03.538
and about seven years later
I came back to play Marius.

00:27:03.622 --> 00:27:05.582
It's been a dream of
mine for a long time

00:27:05.665 --> 00:27:07.834
and to see it coming
true is an amazing thing.

00:27:07.876 --> 00:27:11.129
&gt;&gt; Nick's done a couple of shows
on Broadway, I think he was

00:27:11.171 --> 00:27:15.216
in "Beauty and the Beast" but
the first time I met Nick was

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:19.471
when he was the last Gavroche
which is the little kid

00:27:19.512 --> 00:27:21.681
in "Les Miserables" on Broadway

00:27:21.723 --> 00:27:24.601
when I closed the show the
first time after 18 years

00:27:24.684 --> 00:27:28.813
and he did the last nine months
of the run and I was reminded

00:27:28.855 --> 00:27:31.816
by his father at the
after show party.

00:27:31.858 --> 00:27:34.569
I'd gone up to him and I'd
obviously had a few drinks

00:27:34.653 --> 00:27:38.323
because I didn't remember
saying, you know, to Nick,

00:27:38.365 --> 00:27:39.908
you know, you are really
terrific and I hope

00:27:39.991 --> 00:27:42.702
when you're older you'll come
back to see us for Marius.

00:27:42.786 --> 00:27:46.998
Well even though I didn't
remember it, he did and I'm

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:50.335
out of the blue, I mean I'd
heard that he was interested

00:27:50.377 --> 00:27:51.920
in going to the theatre

00:27:52.003 --> 00:27:54.047
so through another way I
actually approached him

00:27:54.089 --> 00:27:57.133
and then I spoke to his
father and he said no,

00:27:57.217 --> 00:27:58.259
this is something
they would really

00:27:58.343 --> 00:27:59.552
like to try and make happen.

00:27:59.594 --> 00:28:02.764
And then it's a great
credit to the family

00:28:02.847 --> 00:28:05.725
that they have moved heaven
and earth to allow him to come

00:28:05.809 --> 00:28:07.977
and be here and he's
having a great time.

00:28:08.061 --> 00:28:09.813
&gt;&gt; You know, I actually
wasn't as nervous

00:28:09.896 --> 00:28:11.272
as I thought I would be.

00:28:11.356 --> 00:28:12.732
I don't think I was
nervous at all.

00:28:12.774 --> 00:28:17.028
I think that I was really
comfortable and just happy to be

00:28:17.070 --> 00:28:20.740
on that stage and really
trying to become the character.

00:28:20.824 --> 00:28:22.325
&gt;&gt; Well what's interesting is

00:28:22.409 --> 00:28:25.078
that he's actually got
a very mature voice

00:28:25.161 --> 00:28:26.746
for somebody who's still 17.

00:28:26.788 --> 00:28:29.582
And he's not quite 18 yet and

00:28:29.666 --> 00:28:32.836
yet the voice sounds much
older than you'd expect.

00:28:32.919 --> 00:28:37.799
And he's also got, I mean, a
tremendous focus all the way

00:28:37.882 --> 00:28:39.926
through rehearsals and
when I first met him.

00:28:40.009 --> 00:28:44.597
I mean he really, really
knows what to go after

00:28:44.681 --> 00:28:47.684
and he just does it with
no problem, great ease

00:28:47.767 --> 00:28:49.728
and the whole cast adore him.

00:28:49.769 --> 00:28:52.772
&gt;&gt; The crowd was great tonight.

00:28:52.856 --> 00:28:54.941
So incredible to be back
on the "Les Mis" stage.

00:28:55.024 --> 00:28:58.611
Obviously a bit of a different
role this time but still

00:28:58.653 --> 00:29:00.864
that same energy and
the same excitement.

00:29:00.947 --> 00:29:03.700
&gt;&gt; Tickets for the concert sold
out in two and a half hours.

00:29:03.783 --> 00:29:05.326
They'll have 17 different
versions

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:07.787
of the main character Valjean to
choose from and the comic relief

00:29:07.829 --> 00:29:10.999
of Alan Armstrong
and Jenny Galloway.

00:29:11.082 --> 00:29:12.041
&gt;&gt; How many thousands of people?

00:29:12.125 --> 00:29:13.293
&gt;&gt; About 5.

00:29:13.376 --> 00:29:14.794
&gt;&gt; About 5000 people.

00:29:14.878 --> 00:29:15.712
And --

00:29:15.795 --> 00:29:17.589
&gt;&gt; Are you nervous?
&gt;&gt; No.

00:29:17.630 --> 00:29:20.216
&gt;&gt; No. No. Not at all.

00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:22.385
&gt;&gt; I think once you see
all the people and know

00:29:22.469 --> 00:29:25.221
that everybody's
rooting for you and know

00:29:25.305 --> 00:29:29.726
that there is not a hostile
soul in the stalls or in --

00:29:29.809 --> 00:29:32.270
anywhere it's just
going to be fun.

00:29:32.312 --> 00:29:34.355
&gt;&gt; You know, here and there
I'm picking up the accent.

00:29:34.439 --> 00:29:36.524
I sing with one now in the
show, which is kind of funny,

00:29:36.608 --> 00:29:41.863
but it's very interesting
to be here and pick

00:29:41.946 --> 00:29:44.407
up the lingo along the way
and make some new friends

00:29:44.491 --> 00:29:45.658
as well, which has been great.

00:29:45.700 --> 00:29:47.368
I'm really enjoying my stay.

00:29:47.452 --> 00:29:49.287
&gt;&gt; They can come and see
the show here [laughter]

00:29:49.329 --> 00:29:50.497
in the morning at 10:00.

00:29:50.538 --> 00:29:52.165
They can go

00:29:52.248 --> 00:29:55.293
to the matinee at the Queen's
and then the following day turn

00:29:55.335 --> 00:29:58.963
up with the other thousands
and tens of thousands of people

00:29:59.005 --> 00:30:01.800
at the O2 for that
extraordinary concert

00:30:01.841 --> 00:30:04.677
that now has a cast of over 500.

00:30:04.761 --> 00:30:09.015
&gt;&gt; Recently they've started
doing celebrity casting

00:30:09.098 --> 00:30:12.811
in "Les Mis" in London and
we've been lucky enough

00:30:12.852 --> 00:30:15.939
to see possibly some
of our better greats

00:30:16.022 --> 00:30:18.191
that what I call the greats
that are going to come forward

00:30:18.233 --> 00:30:20.276
in the next ten years.

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:24.447
The Lancashire tenor Alfie Boe
has undertaken, not just one

00:30:24.531 --> 00:30:26.032
but three stints in "Les Mis"

00:30:26.074 --> 00:30:28.117
and claims it's his
very favorite role.

00:30:28.201 --> 00:30:29.577
&gt;&gt; I've done the "Les Mis" role.

00:30:29.661 --> 00:30:32.872
I'm going to go back into
"Les Miserables" next --

00:30:32.956 --> 00:30:37.877
this year in June to
do a six-month run.

00:30:37.919 --> 00:30:42.841
But I'm wanting to really
break the genres a little bit,

00:30:42.882 --> 00:30:45.718
you know, people
call it crossover.

00:30:45.802 --> 00:30:48.179
People call it all
sorts of things,

00:30:48.221 --> 00:30:50.640
but I want to just break
the genre and cross

00:30:50.723 --> 00:30:53.935
over to a new audience
not the repertoire so much

00:30:54.018 --> 00:30:57.897
or sing different repertoire
but make sure it's legitimate

00:30:57.939 --> 00:31:01.651
for my voice, you know,
that I'm legitimate enough

00:31:01.734 --> 00:31:03.361
to sing it, you know.

00:31:03.403 --> 00:31:06.573
And still sing it in the same
quality and the same standard

00:31:06.656 --> 00:31:08.408
that I would an operatic
aria in a way.

00:31:08.449 --> 00:31:12.745
&gt;&gt; The comedian, Matt Luke as is
possibly better known for shows

00:31:12.787 --> 00:31:14.497
like "Little Britain"
and "Come Fly with Me"

00:31:14.581 --> 00:31:18.918
and he's actually begged the
producers Cameron Mackintosh's

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:21.254
company for a part
in the musical,

00:31:21.337 --> 00:31:23.923
which of course did nothing
to dent the box office.

00:31:24.007 --> 00:31:27.385
It shot it through the roof
and it was like a new injection

00:31:27.427 --> 00:31:30.847
if you like of vitality
and once again, of course,

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:35.101
make it one of the biggest
take musicals of all time.

00:31:35.143 --> 00:31:38.271
Cameron himself said
that well he's had many,

00:31:38.354 --> 00:31:42.150
many hits he never thought that
he would be known as the man

00:31:42.233 --> 00:31:45.820
who brought "Les Mis" to the
British and Broadway public

00:31:45.904 --> 00:31:49.115
but how lucky we are
that he took that chance.

00:31:49.198 --> 00:31:52.118
&gt;&gt; I mean I was very lucky
because they took me to Broadway

00:31:52.201 --> 00:31:55.788
as well and every musical
performer wants to go

00:31:55.872 --> 00:31:57.999
to Broadway so my
dream came true

00:31:58.082 --> 00:32:02.045
when I was 21, which
was amazing.

00:32:02.128 --> 00:32:06.841
And also my daughter Eliza also
played Cosette at some point

00:32:06.925 --> 00:32:08.134
and I went back into the show

00:32:08.217 --> 00:32:11.137
and I played the same
role Eponine with her.

00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:13.806
So it's emotional for me.

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:15.266
It's like it's my whole life.

00:32:15.308 --> 00:32:18.728
I mean, I think -- I have
to be honest, you know,

00:32:18.811 --> 00:32:22.899
it's the best role I've
ever played and it did --

00:32:22.982 --> 00:32:24.943
you know it gave
me an incredible --

00:32:24.984 --> 00:32:27.946
well people still
know me today from it

00:32:27.987 --> 00:32:31.908
and the album is still selling
that I made 21 years ago.

00:32:31.991 --> 00:32:35.620
So, you know, I -- for
me it's in the heart.

00:32:35.662 --> 00:32:37.664
It's really personal for me.

00:32:37.705 --> 00:32:39.958
&gt;&gt; Now amongst many
of the awards that

00:32:39.999 --> 00:32:42.627
"Les Miserables" has picked up,

00:32:42.669 --> 00:32:45.171
this to me would be
the biggest compliment.

00:32:45.213 --> 00:32:49.509
In 2004, her Majesty,
the Queen, asked the cast

00:32:49.592 --> 00:32:51.344
and everyone connected
to the show

00:32:51.386 --> 00:32:55.181
to stage a very special
version at Windsor Castle

00:32:55.223 --> 00:32:58.351
for then President --
President Jacques Chirac.

00:32:58.393 --> 00:33:00.937
Now can you imagine how nerve
racking that must be first

00:33:01.020 --> 00:33:03.690
of all, but apparently the Queen
thought this was a great idea.

00:33:03.773 --> 00:33:05.274
It was an official visit.

00:33:05.358 --> 00:33:08.528
Lots of people were going to
be invited and apparently,

00:33:08.569 --> 00:33:09.862
sadly there's no record.

00:33:09.946 --> 00:33:11.948
There's no recording of
this particular night.

00:33:12.031 --> 00:33:13.950
I think that this really
should have been recorded

00:33:14.033 --> 00:33:16.035
and taped because, you know,

00:33:16.119 --> 00:33:19.038
just to see our Queen
enjoying this musical

00:33:19.122 --> 00:33:22.333
and she's already been we
can tell you, unofficially,

00:33:22.375 --> 00:33:23.501
many times to see this.

00:33:23.543 --> 00:33:24.711
It's one of her personal
favorites.

00:33:24.794 --> 00:33:26.587
&gt;&gt; You don't expect to
win a Tony when you're

00:33:26.671 --> 00:33:29.007
that age you've seen
it on the TV

00:33:29.048 --> 00:33:30.842
and suddenly you're
told you're going to be

00:33:30.883 --> 00:33:32.385
up for one and then you win it.

00:33:32.468 --> 00:33:33.803
It's just bizarre.

00:33:33.886 --> 00:33:36.014
I mean, I just thought
this is ridiculous.

00:33:36.055 --> 00:33:38.975
I'm here with all these
stars that I've heard about.

00:33:39.058 --> 00:33:42.854
You know, it's just amazing.

00:33:42.895 --> 00:33:45.648
I used to sort of go oh no,
awards don't mean anything

00:33:45.732 --> 00:33:47.692
but now, I look at it
and I'm quite proud.

00:33:47.734 --> 00:33:49.068
[laughter]

00:33:49.110 --> 00:33:51.988
&gt;&gt; Every year it picks
up more and more awards.

00:33:52.071 --> 00:33:54.949
There was a special
10th anniversary show

00:33:55.033 --> 00:33:59.078
at the Royal Albert Hall
and a recent BBC radio poll,

00:33:59.162 --> 00:34:03.583
it was picked out as the
best musical of all time

00:34:03.624 --> 00:34:06.961
and it's hard to imagine that,
you know, when he wrote it,

00:34:07.045 --> 00:34:08.921
whether he would have thought

00:34:09.005 --> 00:34:11.382
that his celebrity
would have carried on.

00:34:11.424 --> 00:34:14.635
I think Victor Hugo would have
thought he wouldn't be known

00:34:14.719 --> 00:34:19.307
in his time but his name is
now plastered across the world.

00:34:19.390 --> 00:34:22.560
It's been seen in
over 30 countries.

00:34:22.602 --> 00:34:25.271
It ran for over ten
years on Broadway.

00:34:25.354 --> 00:34:28.775
You know, when you think about
it in those terms, you know,

00:34:28.858 --> 00:34:30.568
it is something of a phenomenon.

00:34:30.610 --> 00:34:34.072
&gt;&gt; And I'm just about to start
on the film where, where --

00:34:34.113 --> 00:34:38.076
we're now working on -- somebody
is working on the screenplay

00:34:38.117 --> 00:34:40.536
and I'm doing it with Working
Title, which is, you know,

00:34:40.620 --> 00:34:43.915
the great British company that's
one of our best film company

00:34:43.956 --> 00:34:46.793
and I'm really looking
forward to finally getting

00:34:46.876 --> 00:34:48.419
"Les Miserables" out
on the big screen.

00:34:48.461 --> 00:34:52.090
&gt;&gt; "Les Miserables", of course
is now a new smash hit mega

00:34:52.131 --> 00:34:54.926
movie looking at all
sorts of gold dust,

00:34:54.967 --> 00:34:57.553
from Baftas to Globes to Oscars.

00:34:57.637 --> 00:34:59.138
But it's not the first time.

00:34:59.222 --> 00:35:01.724
There are already ten
silent versions made

00:35:01.808 --> 00:35:03.559
of this particular story.

00:35:03.643 --> 00:35:05.144
There's been a radio version.

00:35:05.186 --> 00:35:07.021
There's been a television
version

00:35:07.105 --> 00:35:09.107
with Anthony Perkins no less,

00:35:09.148 --> 00:35:10.942
which was kind of
warmly received.

00:35:10.983 --> 00:35:19.408
[ Music, gunshots, screaming ]

00:35:46.185 --> 00:35:47.812
It's one of those
stories that time

00:35:47.854 --> 00:35:51.399
and time again people re-look
at and think they can retell

00:35:51.482 --> 00:35:55.111
but nobody really could
have even predicted

00:35:55.194 --> 00:35:58.614
or had the slightest hint that
the stage and Broadway version,

00:35:58.698 --> 00:36:01.409
the West End [inaudible]
Broadway version would be

00:36:01.492 --> 00:36:02.660
so phenomenal.

00:36:02.702 --> 00:36:05.621
And of course, you know when
people said first of all

00:36:05.705 --> 00:36:07.290
when it opened in London,

00:36:07.373 --> 00:36:10.251
how can you possibly think
you're going to have a smash hit

00:36:10.334 --> 00:36:13.296
with a title which is basically
all about being miserable,

00:36:13.379 --> 00:36:16.674
"Les Miserables",
how wrong they were.

00:36:16.716 --> 00:36:20.261
&gt;&gt; The film will actually use
the music, the wonderful music

00:36:20.344 --> 00:36:23.306
from the show but tell the
story in cinematic terms.

00:36:23.389 --> 00:36:27.393
I mean what we have here is a
wonderful theatrical version

00:36:27.435 --> 00:36:31.606
but you know, the cinema we
will reinvent it yet again

00:36:31.689 --> 00:36:33.107
to tell it for the cinema.

00:36:33.191 --> 00:36:35.526
&gt;&gt; What was fantastic
on this was the chance

00:36:35.568 --> 00:36:41.699
to reunite the original creative
team that created the musical.

00:36:41.741 --> 00:36:46.412
So, you know, I wanted to
create a new song for the film

00:36:46.454 --> 00:36:48.539
that talked to -- that
was inspired by the novel

00:36:48.581 --> 00:36:52.543
which talks about the
extraordinary epiphany

00:36:52.585 --> 00:36:56.839
of what Jean Valjean has when
he feels this incredible love

00:36:56.923 --> 00:36:58.758
for this child that's
come into his life

00:36:58.799 --> 00:37:02.595
and so I asked Claude-Michel
Schonberg and Alain Boublil

00:37:02.637 --> 00:37:07.433
if they could come up with a
new song and I had Alain Boublil

00:37:07.475 --> 00:37:11.354
and Herby Kretzmer the lyricist,
you know, writing a new piece

00:37:11.437 --> 00:37:14.398
for me and that was incredibly
exciting to get to work

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:17.276
with the original
composer on the music.

00:37:17.360 --> 00:37:19.779
&gt;&gt; I'm Poppie what a
pleasure to meet you.

00:37:19.862 --> 00:37:21.822
&gt;&gt; What's your name?
&gt;&gt; Poppie.

00:37:21.906 --> 00:37:23.407
&gt;&gt; Hi Poppie. How are you?

00:37:23.449 --> 00:37:26.202
&gt;&gt; I mean the film
was truly beautiful.

00:37:26.285 --> 00:37:28.913
I cried all the way through it.
I loved it very much.

00:37:28.955 --> 00:37:32.458
Now what was your first
experience of "Les Mis"?

00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:37.296
&gt;&gt; I saw it here in London but
I didn't see it until Cameron

00:37:37.380 --> 00:37:39.966
and Tom were already making
the movie and they talked to me

00:37:40.049 --> 00:37:43.177
about it and so I was on
my way to Italy actually

00:37:43.261 --> 00:37:44.637
for a friend's wedding.

00:37:44.679 --> 00:37:45.763
And so I stopped in to see it

00:37:45.805 --> 00:37:47.306
and we had a couple
of meetings about it.

00:37:47.390 --> 00:37:50.309
&gt;&gt; What was the biggest
challenge going into the parts?

00:37:50.393 --> 00:37:52.812
&gt;&gt; Well I think the
challenges actually unfolded

00:37:52.895 --> 00:37:54.355
as we went along, you know.

00:37:54.438 --> 00:37:58.276
It wasn't quite clear how
challenging it was going to be

00:37:58.317 --> 00:38:00.695
in the beginning because
it's very easy to say oh

00:38:00.778 --> 00:38:02.405
and by the way you're
going to sing every take.

00:38:02.488 --> 00:38:06.242
But then the actual reality of
doing that and the discipline

00:38:06.325 --> 00:38:07.743
that has to come into your life

00:38:07.827 --> 00:38:10.997
to keep yourself available every
day and your voice available

00:38:11.080 --> 00:38:14.500
and Tom as the director
is greedy.

00:38:14.583 --> 00:38:16.711
You know, he'll shoot and he'll
shoot and he'll shoot and,

00:38:16.794 --> 00:38:19.755
you know, and every take
we did was the full length

00:38:19.839 --> 00:38:23.551
of the song as well.

00:38:23.634 --> 00:38:25.678
So yeah, I mean, you
know, ultimately though,

00:38:25.720 --> 00:38:27.513
this was an incredibly
enjoyable experience.

00:38:27.555 --> 00:38:31.642
&gt;&gt; I grew up with the -- my
mom performing in the show

00:38:31.684 --> 00:38:32.727
in the first national tour.

00:38:32.810 --> 00:38:34.145
She understudied Fantine.

00:38:34.186 --> 00:38:35.563
It's actually one
of my first memories

00:38:35.646 --> 00:38:36.981
of seeing the show is seeing
my mother play the part

00:38:37.023 --> 00:38:38.524
that I play in the film.

00:38:38.607 --> 00:38:42.069
&gt;&gt; I saw it when I was beginning
to think about making the film

00:38:42.153 --> 00:38:44.322
and then I saw it
four or five times

00:38:44.363 --> 00:38:47.283
and what was extraordinary is
that after the second time,

00:38:47.366 --> 00:38:48.993
the melodies were just in
my head and that was it.

00:38:49.035 --> 00:38:52.204
I mean, for the last year and
a half it's been going around

00:38:52.246 --> 00:38:53.456
and around and around.

00:38:53.539 --> 00:38:55.374
I mean they were
extraordinarily addictive.

00:38:55.416 --> 00:38:57.626
&gt;&gt; Isabelle Alan.

00:38:57.710 --> 00:38:58.919
[cheering]

00:38:59.003 --> 00:39:01.130
&gt;&gt; A year ago she was
performing on stage at school.

00:39:01.213 --> 00:39:02.673
&gt;&gt; She's just so gorgeous.

00:39:02.715 --> 00:39:03.924
It was her first movie.

00:39:04.008 --> 00:39:06.010
She has no idea how
brilliant she is.

00:39:06.052 --> 00:39:07.386
She's just so natural.

00:39:07.470 --> 00:39:10.264
&gt;&gt; It may be a lot for a
ten-year-old to take in,

00:39:10.348 --> 00:39:12.850
but Isabelle looks set to
walk many more red carpets

00:39:12.892 --> 00:39:14.018
in the future.

00:39:14.060 --> 00:39:15.394
&gt;&gt; We did everything
in one take.

00:39:15.436 --> 00:39:20.900
Tom, Tom left a few of
the songs the solos kind

00:39:20.941 --> 00:39:23.110
of in one long continuous take.

00:39:23.194 --> 00:39:25.237
Did very few cuts in it.

00:39:25.321 --> 00:39:27.740
What he did was he
filmed most everything

00:39:27.782 --> 00:39:30.451
from a multi-camera approach,

00:39:30.534 --> 00:39:33.120
which meant that you could
do a whole take knowing

00:39:33.204 --> 00:39:34.622
that the entire performance
was captured

00:39:34.705 --> 00:39:36.499
because it would have been
very hard, I think especially

00:39:36.582 --> 00:39:39.752
with the singing being live
to go from one take to another

00:39:39.794 --> 00:39:42.254
like an hour can change
your voice, you know?

00:39:42.338 --> 00:39:44.590
It's the time of
day, the barometer.

00:39:44.632 --> 00:39:45.674
There's so many factors.

00:39:45.758 --> 00:39:48.344
So Tom needed to be
able to capture all

00:39:48.427 --> 00:39:50.388
of the performances live
in order to edit in a way

00:39:50.429 --> 00:39:52.098
that wouldn't become
distracting.

00:39:52.181 --> 00:39:53.391
&gt;&gt; I didn't want
to make the film

00:39:53.432 --> 00:39:56.602
if I didn't have my Jean
Valjean and Hugh came in

00:39:56.644 --> 00:40:00.648
and made me realize that
I could make this film.

00:40:00.731 --> 00:40:03.484
I mean because I'm short
just this one person.

00:40:03.567 --> 00:40:06.112
I mean who else could
sing at that level act

00:40:06.195 --> 00:40:07.738
in such an extraordinary way.

00:40:07.780 --> 00:40:11.450
He's a bona fided film star in
the old sense can carry a film.

00:40:11.534 --> 00:40:14.495
Has a requisite strength to
play a famously strong man

00:40:14.578 --> 00:40:18.874
but also has the grace and kind
of the spiritual gentleness

00:40:18.958 --> 00:40:20.584
that the character needs.

00:40:20.626 --> 00:40:21.919
It's Hugh Jackman.

00:40:21.961 --> 00:40:24.964
&gt;&gt; Tom who at first day of
rehearsal said I need you --

00:40:25.005 --> 00:40:27.883
actually before then he said
I need you unrecognizable.

00:40:27.967 --> 00:40:31.303
He said if people recognize you,
I want them to think you're sick

00:40:31.345 --> 00:40:33.764
in real life so I did my best.

00:40:33.806 --> 00:40:37.351
&gt;&gt; He went on a really
hard-core journey to try

00:40:37.435 --> 00:40:39.478
to physically capture
what it'd be

00:40:39.520 --> 00:40:42.273
like to be a convict
of 20 years standing.

00:40:42.314 --> 00:40:46.819
He lost 30-pounds of weight
and we shaved his head.

00:40:46.902 --> 00:40:49.655
I mean we talk a lot about
shaving or cutting Annie's hair

00:40:49.738 --> 00:40:52.408
but Hugh Jackman's hair
was shaved very brutally.

00:40:52.491 --> 00:40:55.619
And then in the opening
scene, I mean this is

00:40:55.661 --> 00:40:57.371
where he's kind of
quite eccentric.

00:40:57.455 --> 00:40:59.999
He went on a 36-hour water fast,

00:41:00.082 --> 00:41:03.878
which means no water
for 36 hours.

00:41:03.961 --> 00:41:05.880
And then was willing to
stand up to his chest

00:41:05.963 --> 00:41:09.341
in seawater putting a
massive [inaudible] on a rope

00:41:09.425 --> 00:41:12.761
so that your skin kind of
went back against his cheeks.

00:41:12.845 --> 00:41:14.930
You know, a lot of us
don't recognize him

00:41:15.014 --> 00:41:16.432
in the opening scene.

00:41:16.515 --> 00:41:21.353
I mean he's so kind of
broken looking by this thing.

00:41:21.437 --> 00:41:23.856
I mean and then when
his character kind

00:41:23.939 --> 00:41:26.567
of flourishes he then put on
weight and he changed again.

00:41:26.650 --> 00:41:29.862
And I mean like his
commitment to this was total.

00:41:29.904 --> 00:41:32.990
&gt;&gt; Yeah. My trainer on this
movie incredible guy David

00:41:33.032 --> 00:41:38.245
Kingsbury has at one point
in his life was a bodybuilder

00:41:38.329 --> 00:41:40.581
and he knew of this
water restriction thing

00:41:40.664 --> 00:41:43.542
which is what bodybuilders do
before a show and you can lose

00:41:43.584 --> 00:41:45.419
up to 10-pounds of body weight.

00:41:45.503 --> 00:41:48.589
And right off the surface,
do not do this at home.

00:41:48.672 --> 00:41:52.134
Trust me and so it
really sinks in your --

00:41:52.218 --> 00:41:55.137
I was already quite skinny
but it makes it quite drastic.

00:41:55.221 --> 00:41:56.639
Your eyes get sunken.

00:41:56.722 --> 00:41:59.558
I look like hell basically
and trust me the headache

00:41:59.600 --> 00:42:01.101
after about 20 hours is
something I don't want

00:42:01.185 --> 00:42:02.353
to repeat.

00:42:02.394 --> 00:42:06.815
&gt;&gt; I was up in Stratford
doing [inaudible]

00:42:06.899 --> 00:42:11.904
and I received a phone call
from my agent telling me

00:42:11.987 --> 00:42:14.323
that I would be playing the
[inaudible] hero Thenardier

00:42:14.406 --> 00:42:15.991
singing master of the house.

00:42:16.075 --> 00:42:23.499
[ Background noise ]

00:42:23.582 --> 00:42:25.292
&gt;&gt; Just like all the singing,
all the tears were done live.

00:42:25.376 --> 00:42:30.589
[laughter] They were
-- they weren't mine.

00:42:30.673 --> 00:42:34.343
I mean I was definitely in
a fragile place that day

00:42:34.426 --> 00:42:36.679
because my hair was
going to be gone.

00:42:36.762 --> 00:42:40.849
But no I really did try to
kind of stay with Fantine

00:42:40.933 --> 00:42:43.894
and Victor Hugo has this
wonderful passage in the book

00:42:43.936 --> 00:42:46.313
where he talks about
Fantine really didn't want

00:42:46.397 --> 00:42:47.773
to cut her hair and how
she receives a letter

00:42:47.815 --> 00:42:50.317
from Thenardier saying that
Cosette doesn't have a skirt

00:42:50.401 --> 00:42:51.735
for winter, she's
going to be cold.

00:42:51.777 --> 00:42:53.779
And she sat there crumbling
the letter in her hand

00:42:53.821 --> 00:42:57.157
for an entire day and then
just like around sunset goes

00:42:57.241 --> 00:42:59.451
down to the wigmaker and says
take it -- how much for it?

00:42:59.535 --> 00:43:01.579
He said 10 francs and
she says cut it off then.

00:43:01.620 --> 00:43:03.622
And she sat there and trying

00:43:03.706 --> 00:43:05.833
to be stoic thinking I'm
clothing my hair with --

00:43:05.916 --> 00:43:07.585
I'm clothing my child
with my hair.

00:43:07.626 --> 00:43:10.421
And I just kind of tried to
bring some of that grace into it

00:43:10.462 --> 00:43:11.755
and not just have
it all be about me.

00:43:11.797 --> 00:43:12.965
[laughter]

00:43:15.926 --> 00:43:19.680
&gt;&gt; For me like any
physical transformation

00:43:19.763 --> 00:43:22.641
that you undergo is obviously
going to be difficult

00:43:22.683 --> 00:43:26.645
but I couldn't take it too
seriously because it was my idea

00:43:26.729 --> 00:43:28.439
to lose my hair and
it was my idea to go

00:43:28.480 --> 00:43:30.316
to the extreme physical
place that I did for this

00:43:30.357 --> 00:43:31.650
and I did it for my job.

00:43:31.692 --> 00:43:33.652
And a lot of people don't
have a choice about it.

00:43:33.694 --> 00:43:35.321
A lot of people get sick
and they lose weight.

00:43:35.404 --> 00:43:37.531
They get sick and they
have to lose their hair.

00:43:37.615 --> 00:43:40.993
&gt;&gt; Tom Hooper who's not a
musical theatre director before

00:43:41.076 --> 00:43:44.622
he went to see the show and
really fell in love with it

00:43:44.663 --> 00:43:48.959
and he said how do I
recreate that feeling

00:43:49.001 --> 00:43:49.960
that you get in the theatre?

00:43:50.002 --> 00:43:52.755
And so he wanted to sing live

00:43:52.838 --> 00:43:55.132
and what he does
actually he's enhanced the

00:43:55.174 --> 00:43:56.717
theatrical experience.

00:43:56.800 --> 00:43:59.720
For example, Anne Hathaway,
her song "I Dreamed a Dream",

00:43:59.803 --> 00:44:03.682
which of course everybody feels
they know, is a single shot

00:44:03.724 --> 00:44:07.561
without cutting for three
minutes of her in close up.

00:44:07.645 --> 00:44:09.938
It's one of the great
performances ever given

00:44:10.022 --> 00:44:14.652
and I think and you never cut
away from it so it's like being

00:44:14.693 --> 00:44:16.612
in the theatre with
the best seat.

00:44:16.695 --> 00:44:19.281
You couldn't even get that seat
in the theatre it's like sitting

00:44:19.365 --> 00:44:21.116
in her lap and listening
to it, you know.

00:44:21.200 --> 00:44:24.495
&gt;&gt; There wasn't anything about
this job that wasn't daunting.

00:44:24.536 --> 00:44:28.165
I mean we were all just kind
of this mixture of anxiety

00:44:28.207 --> 00:44:34.338
and gratitude and excitement and
nerves and -- but you know --

00:44:34.380 --> 00:44:36.674
and then of course you show up
on set and you're just like --

00:44:36.715 --> 00:44:39.718
because we've all been working
in our own lives on the music.

00:44:39.760 --> 00:44:42.721
And you show up and
you hear Hugh Jackman,

00:44:42.763 --> 00:44:43.889
and of course we
kind of all knew

00:44:43.931 --> 00:44:45.599
that Hugh could sing
but not like this.

00:44:45.683 --> 00:44:49.687
And then Eddie Redmayne with the
most gorgeous voice just coming

00:44:49.728 --> 00:44:54.608
out of nowhere so I mean,
so it was exciting to work

00:44:54.692 --> 00:44:55.943
with everybody to hear what
they were doing but I think,

00:44:56.026 --> 00:44:58.278
you know, hearing how
talented everyone else was kind

00:44:58.362 --> 00:45:00.906
of pushed us all on
-- me on at least.

00:45:02.700 --> 00:45:06.078
&gt;&gt; So many auditions.
Six in total.

00:45:06.120 --> 00:45:09.081
Three on tape, three in person.
Two flights to London.

00:45:09.123 --> 00:45:12.042
[ Background noise ]

00:45:15.003 --> 00:45:17.047
&gt;&gt; You know 60 million people
have seen it around the world.

00:45:17.089 --> 00:45:20.759
London has taken it to their
hearts in the very beginning.

00:45:20.843 --> 00:45:25.764
I wanted to protect what
it is people love about it

00:45:25.806 --> 00:45:28.934
and somehow take that and
intensify it on the big screen

00:45:29.017 --> 00:45:31.812
and I think it's how it makes
people feel and how people --

00:45:31.895 --> 00:45:34.690
you go back to re-experience
these strong emotions.

00:45:34.773 --> 00:45:36.942
I wanted to make the emotions
they feel even more intense

00:45:37.025 --> 00:45:38.360
on the big screen.

00:45:38.444 --> 00:45:41.822
&gt;&gt; I don't think I think about
it in terms of hard or easy.

00:45:41.905 --> 00:45:44.783
It's just my job.

00:45:44.867 --> 00:45:48.787
So you know, it's not like it
all kind of a feeling comes

00:45:48.871 --> 00:45:51.915
to you in one great moment and
you're like, oh, I'm quite good

00:45:51.957 --> 00:45:53.959
at this or oh, I'm
terrible at this.

00:45:54.001 --> 00:45:57.004
You're so far removed from
it and at that point I'd done

00:45:57.087 --> 00:45:59.757
so much preparation
that I was so into it,

00:45:59.798 --> 00:46:02.593
it wasn't really
about performing.

00:46:02.634 --> 00:46:05.596
It was more about just
honoring what I had learned

00:46:05.637 --> 00:46:07.765
in the research that I
had done and just trying

00:46:07.806 --> 00:46:11.143
to open myself wide enough
to let all that come out.

00:46:11.226 --> 00:46:16.815
[ Background noise ]

00:46:16.857 --> 00:46:22.821
&gt;&gt; Really amazing
to be up there.

00:46:22.863 --> 00:46:25.824
It's so cool.

00:46:25.908 --> 00:46:28.660
&gt;&gt; Well John Valjean,
for actors it's a bit

00:46:28.702 --> 00:46:29.995
like a Hamlet or something.

00:46:30.037 --> 00:46:33.582
It is a kind of role
you dream of playing

00:46:33.665 --> 00:46:35.334
and also secretly
terrified of playing

00:46:35.375 --> 00:46:39.838
because it requires
everything of you

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:42.299
and with John Valjean
emotionally, physically

00:46:42.341 --> 00:46:46.470
and vocally everyday it requires
it usually all at the same time.

00:46:46.512 --> 00:46:49.515
So it was an incredible
opportunity and I'm just

00:46:49.556 --> 00:46:52.601
so lucky to have been
working with Tom Hooper

00:46:52.684 --> 00:46:54.061
and all these great
actors, you know?

00:46:54.144 --> 00:46:57.731
&gt;&gt; You know, I used to go to
the Empire Leicester Square

00:46:57.815 --> 00:47:01.401
and the Odeon Leicester
Square as a kid and dream

00:47:01.485 --> 00:47:03.779
of one day having a film
maybe in those cinemas.

00:47:03.862 --> 00:47:06.782
I remember the ritual as I
left I'd always turn back

00:47:06.865 --> 00:47:09.034
and watch the end credits roll
and make a kind of commitment

00:47:09.076 --> 00:47:11.995
to myself to try to get a
film on one of those screens.

00:47:12.037 --> 00:47:14.122
To have both screens at
the premier last night

00:47:14.206 --> 00:47:17.125
with 3000 people was a kind
of great, I don't know,

00:47:17.209 --> 00:47:21.296
homecoming for me as a Londoner
to have that opportunity.

00:47:21.380 --> 00:47:22.381
&gt;&gt; You know he would have
everyone over when we were

00:47:22.464 --> 00:47:24.842
in London and it was great.

00:47:24.883 --> 00:47:29.388
He has a very, very great estate
over there and it was a lot

00:47:29.471 --> 00:47:32.933
of fun and it was nice after
long weeks for everyone

00:47:33.016 --> 00:47:36.186
to come together and do that
and he's just a great guy.

00:47:39.064 --> 00:47:40.816
&gt;&gt; At the beginning, it's
a very embarrassing thing

00:47:40.899 --> 00:47:42.484
to actually sing to people.

00:47:42.568 --> 00:47:45.779
It's very exposing thing to
be like [inaudible] on the set

00:47:45.863 --> 00:47:48.323
and so when you get -- you're
allowed to get so relaxed

00:47:48.407 --> 00:47:52.494
around each other and learn to
-- and you didn't feel awkward.

00:47:55.414 --> 00:47:58.375
&gt;&gt; There have been subsequently
some wild singing parties.

00:47:58.417 --> 00:48:00.878
We were down in a pub in New
York the other day and Russell

00:48:00.919 --> 00:48:02.087
and Russell's mate with a guitar

00:48:02.129 --> 00:48:04.423
and we were all singing
-- singing songs.

00:48:04.464 --> 00:48:08.427
It's -- I've never quite gotten
over the fear factor of it

00:48:08.510 --> 00:48:10.012
but it is quite entertaining.

00:48:10.095 --> 00:48:10.971
&gt;&gt; You know what?

00:48:11.054 --> 00:48:12.556
I feel so proud to
be part of this.

00:48:12.598 --> 00:48:15.934
It was so odd last night, we
had the premier and was ended

00:48:16.018 --> 00:48:18.937
up driving back to -- there
was a party afterwards and sort

00:48:19.021 --> 00:48:22.274
of drove through Leicester
Square and up [inaudible] past

00:48:22.316 --> 00:48:23.901
where "Les Mis" is on.

00:48:23.942 --> 00:48:26.653
And there in the Palace Theatre
where it used to play I have

00:48:26.737 --> 00:48:28.280
so many memories as
a kid of walking up

00:48:28.322 --> 00:48:32.993
and down there wanting to
maybe be an actor but never was

00:48:33.076 --> 00:48:35.162
in my wildest dreams I'd get
to be a part of it so yeah.

00:48:35.245 --> 00:48:36.580
I feel very privileged.

00:48:36.622 --> 00:48:38.457
&gt;&gt; You dreamed a dream.
It came true.

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:39.458
[laughter]
&gt;&gt; Oh, that's so Jesus.

00:48:39.499 --> 00:48:40.959
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:48:41.043 --> 00:48:42.544
&gt;&gt; You may not have been the
first person to say that.

00:48:42.628 --> 00:48:43.795
[laughter]

00:48:43.837 --> 00:48:46.673
&gt;&gt; See Russell loves
a sing-along

00:48:46.757 --> 00:48:48.216
and if you ever get
invited to his place

00:48:48.300 --> 00:48:50.886
for a party Lucrezia right

00:48:50.969 --> 00:48:54.348
after you do David Letterman
then make sure you take a song

00:48:54.431 --> 00:48:55.849
because he gets everyone
singing.

00:48:55.933 --> 00:48:58.393
But it was a great
way to let off steam.

00:48:58.477 --> 00:49:01.313
I mean, we were all -- you
were not allowed to sing any

00:49:01.355 --> 00:49:04.483
of your songs from your
repertoire in the musical.

00:49:04.524 --> 00:49:06.151
That was the only rule.

00:49:06.234 --> 00:49:08.862
&gt;&gt; It's -- you know it's easy
to see why Eponine loves Marius

00:49:08.946 --> 00:49:10.864
when he looks like Eddie
Redmayne, you know.

00:49:10.948 --> 00:49:13.575
But then equally it's easy
to see why he loves Cosette

00:49:13.659 --> 00:49:15.953
when it's Amanda
Seyfried to be fair.

00:49:15.994 --> 00:49:16.912
[laughter]

00:49:16.995 --> 00:49:18.205
&gt;&gt; Yeah, no, there was a lot --

00:49:18.288 --> 00:49:20.332
the thing about this film
because it's embarrassing

00:49:20.374 --> 00:49:22.960
because we're all such
aggressive "Les Mis" fans.

00:49:23.001 --> 00:49:26.797
It's the first film I've seen
when people would come in to set

00:49:26.838 --> 00:49:30.842
on their days off to kind
of see how everyone's doing.

00:49:30.926 --> 00:49:32.970
[laughter] No one
to miss out so yeah,

00:49:33.011 --> 00:49:34.805
we were all very supportive.

00:49:34.846 --> 00:49:38.809
&gt;&gt; Well, I think it's,
you know, when you --

00:49:38.850 --> 00:49:40.686
I've always admired
Hugh Jackman.

00:49:40.769 --> 00:49:42.813
I've always respected
his career and his work.

00:49:42.854 --> 00:49:47.234
I think he's just got
fantastic range in what he does.

00:49:47.317 --> 00:49:51.488
I just think the world
of him professionally and

00:49:51.530 --> 00:49:53.365
so then, you know,
I was thinking,

00:49:53.407 --> 00:49:56.201
well you know you meet someone
who's almost like an idol

00:49:56.243 --> 00:49:57.327
and you think they're
never going

00:49:57.369 --> 00:49:58.996
to amount to that expectation.

00:49:59.037 --> 00:50:00.664
Well here's the thing
with Hugh Jackman,

00:50:00.706 --> 00:50:02.374
he didn't match it,
he raised it.

00:50:02.457 --> 00:50:05.043
I just didn't think
somebody could be so cool

00:50:05.127 --> 00:50:08.547
with all the success
he's had to be this --

00:50:08.588 --> 00:50:10.632
the most levelheaded person.

00:50:10.716 --> 00:50:12.426
He is so calm on set.

00:50:12.509 --> 00:50:14.011
He's like hi.

00:50:14.052 --> 00:50:17.014
He's lovely and then
it's like action and this

00:50:17.055 --> 00:50:21.101
like character busts out of
his soul and it's like wow.

00:50:21.184 --> 00:50:23.395
And for me that's inspiring
at the beginning of my career

00:50:23.437 --> 00:50:25.939
to meet somebody like that
who's got that perfect balance.

00:50:26.023 --> 00:50:28.400
I look at -- you know, he's
inspiring to me definitely.

00:50:28.442 --> 00:50:33.238
&gt;&gt; I spent the most time
with Eddie, I think. Yes.

00:50:33.280 --> 00:50:37.743
&gt;&gt; And who did you bond with
the most, would you say?

00:50:37.826 --> 00:50:41.580
&gt;&gt; I guess Eddie.
I guess I bonded -- no.

00:50:41.621 --> 00:50:43.248
I don't know -- yeah.

00:50:43.290 --> 00:50:49.046
I guess Eddie for the most part
I bonded with Eddie and Samantha

00:50:49.087 --> 00:50:53.967
but we all kind of had -- I
mean we all had scenes together

00:50:54.051 --> 00:50:56.511
and throughout rehearsal
we were able to kind of get

00:50:56.595 --> 00:51:01.058
to know each other and bond
through like the terror

00:51:01.099 --> 00:51:03.018
of singing live and
also the fact

00:51:03.101 --> 00:51:05.520
that we were all major,
major "Les Mis" fans.

00:51:05.604 --> 00:51:06.938
And the fact that we all worked,

00:51:07.022 --> 00:51:09.274
like our ass off
to get the roles.

00:51:09.357 --> 00:51:14.654
So but I guess I spent the most
time with Eddie and Samantha

00:51:14.738 --> 00:51:16.073
and it was a nice little group.

00:51:16.114 --> 00:51:19.618
&gt;&gt; I think it was
just the difference

00:51:19.701 --> 00:51:22.037
in how you convey this emotion.

00:51:22.120 --> 00:51:25.040
I think in the theatre you're
projecting to 2000 seats

00:51:25.123 --> 00:51:26.833
and everything is heightened

00:51:26.917 --> 00:51:29.127
because there's no point
having a little private moment

00:51:29.169 --> 00:51:31.129
on stage you need people
to connect with that story

00:51:31.171 --> 00:51:35.175
and to sort of feel what
your character's feeling

00:51:35.258 --> 00:51:39.137
and on screen the camera's so
close to you that you feel,

00:51:39.221 --> 00:51:41.890
you know, it's so intimate you
can be more truthful and that

00:51:41.973 --> 00:51:43.767
that realism is amazing.

00:51:43.809 --> 00:51:45.602
But then you can't beat
the live buzz of theatre

00:51:45.644 --> 00:51:49.648
so what I feel is exciting
about this project certainly

00:51:49.689 --> 00:51:53.276
from an acting point of view
is combining those two together

00:51:53.318 --> 00:51:56.822
so it's kind of that utter
realism of film but with

00:51:56.905 --> 00:51:59.324
that kind of live buzz
you get in theater.

00:51:59.407 --> 00:52:02.160
It's combining the two to create
something that feels unique.

00:52:02.202 --> 00:52:05.330
&gt;&gt; I had to sing.
Just had to sing.

00:52:05.413 --> 00:52:10.168
I was completely, completely
out of my comfort zone

00:52:10.210 --> 00:52:14.131
and you know very, very
insecure about my voice.

00:52:14.172 --> 00:52:16.216
I definitely wish
I'd had more time

00:52:16.299 --> 00:52:24.182
to prepare the soprano technique
but you know, I did my best

00:52:24.266 --> 00:52:26.351
and I feel like, you know,
this is the kind of the --

00:52:26.434 --> 00:52:30.063
it's a new unique experience
and the opportunity's not going

00:52:30.147 --> 00:52:34.317
to come around very often so
I definitely think this is,

00:52:34.359 --> 00:52:36.069
you know, the coolest
thing I've ever been in.

00:52:36.153 --> 00:52:40.240
&gt;&gt; Well I didn't get a call.
That was the first step.

00:52:40.323 --> 00:52:44.035
Basically I was performing
as Nancy in "Oliver" on stage

00:52:44.119 --> 00:52:46.997
and Cameron Mackintosh,
the producer, walked on

00:52:47.038 --> 00:52:51.042
and announced to a live
audience, yeah, that I was going

00:52:51.084 --> 00:52:53.211
to be playing Eponine
-- that was my face.

00:52:53.295 --> 00:52:55.547
Right there.
That was my reaction.

00:52:55.630 --> 00:52:57.465
And it's on YouTube.

00:52:57.549 --> 00:52:59.968
I've watched it since
because I can't remember it.

00:53:00.051 --> 00:53:02.053
I just remember hearing Eponine.

00:53:02.137 --> 00:53:05.223
I remember my heart nearly
falling out of my chest

00:53:05.307 --> 00:53:07.142
and going -- because I've
never wanted anything more.

00:53:07.225 --> 00:53:10.270
I never -- this has been
a part for four years.

00:53:10.353 --> 00:53:14.191
It's a character I feel so
passionate about and oh gosh

00:53:14.232 --> 00:53:16.735
and to get that news
in that way,

00:53:16.776 --> 00:53:18.111
that was my reaction
right there.

00:53:18.195 --> 00:53:19.988
You did a really good
impression of me.

00:53:20.071 --> 00:53:23.533
&gt;&gt; It's like my inner,
you know teenager.

00:53:23.575 --> 00:53:26.745
You grew up with Ali G
and all those things --

00:53:26.786 --> 00:53:31.082
I was properly star struck
by Sasha and he arrived onset

00:53:31.166 --> 00:53:33.168
at a point that he Helena
Bonham-Carter arrived at a point

00:53:33.251 --> 00:53:35.253
which we'd being going through
this rigorous quite sort

00:53:35.295 --> 00:53:39.257
of intense process forever
and they arrived in sort

00:53:39.299 --> 00:53:42.385
of a lightness arrived
and it made me very happy.

00:53:42.427 --> 00:53:43.803
It was just -- I
was a bit of a sort

00:53:43.887 --> 00:53:47.140
of a lifetime aspiration
was to --

00:53:47.224 --> 00:53:48.767
I never thought I'd get the
opportunity to work with him

00:53:48.808 --> 00:53:50.268
but it was pretty
wonderful to do so.

00:53:50.352 --> 00:53:54.272
&gt;&gt; I think I was excited about
there was a role I knew nothing

00:53:54.314 --> 00:53:56.983
about at least I kind
of -- I knew this girl.

00:53:57.067 --> 00:54:00.612
I knew this piece, I felt
so passionately about it

00:54:00.654 --> 00:54:03.073
so to be -- that was the
one comforting thing,

00:54:03.114 --> 00:54:04.532
I think about entering it.

00:54:04.616 --> 00:54:07.118
I wanted to work with these
actors that I'd, you know,

00:54:07.202 --> 00:54:09.287
admired for years, people
like Hugh Jackman, you know?

00:54:09.329 --> 00:54:11.539
And Eddie Redmayne I knew
had been cast as Marius

00:54:11.623 --> 00:54:13.291
and when I was auditioning

00:54:13.333 --> 00:54:15.627
for Eponine there were posters
everywhere with "My Week

00:54:15.669 --> 00:54:18.213
With Marilyn" and I was
like, remember being just

00:54:18.296 --> 00:54:21.007
like positive thinking he
could be my Marius and working

00:54:21.091 --> 00:54:24.302
with people like that,
that was such a highlight.

00:54:24.386 --> 00:54:26.596
&gt;&gt; I was lucky enough to attend
many of these gala nights

00:54:26.638 --> 00:54:29.516
of "Les Mis" and they've
always been magical.

00:54:29.599 --> 00:54:31.351
You know, any musical

00:54:31.434 --> 00:54:35.480
that celebrates a particular
milestone is always sort

00:54:35.522 --> 00:54:38.316
of like one of those moments
where you think it's been going

00:54:38.358 --> 00:54:40.944
so long can you really
inject something into that?

00:54:40.986 --> 00:54:43.655
And it does have
that magical touch.

00:54:43.738 --> 00:54:46.366
I think what's also
interesting is the fact

00:54:46.449 --> 00:54:49.369
that as somebody who's seen it
as I say quite a few times now,

00:54:49.452 --> 00:54:52.163
you go along and each time
there's always something

00:54:52.205 --> 00:54:54.457
that you don't quite remember

00:54:54.499 --> 00:54:57.377
from the first time
or the second time.

00:54:57.460 --> 00:55:01.298
And you do come away it's what
they call one of those musicals;

00:55:01.339 --> 00:55:04.342
it's what they call
a memorable musical.

00:55:04.426 --> 00:55:08.305
Not just because of the story
but particularly with the songs.

00:55:08.346 --> 00:55:12.309
You do go out and days later
you've got this fantastic melody

00:55:12.350 --> 00:55:15.145
in your head and you're thinking
to yourself, what is that?

00:55:15.186 --> 00:55:16.688
What is that?
Because a with a lot of songs

00:55:16.771 --> 00:55:19.774
from musicals you don't always
know the title but you only have

00:55:19.858 --> 00:55:23.528
to hear, you know, the sort
of words of "On My Own"

00:55:23.570 --> 00:55:27.449
and it strikes a chord to
any generation and I do kind

00:55:27.532 --> 00:55:31.244
of think that that's why it's
been covered by so many people.

00:55:31.328 --> 00:55:34.664
So many people resonate with the
lyrics and the beautiful melody

00:55:34.706 --> 00:55:38.460
of many of the songs from
the score of "Les Mis"

00:55:38.543 --> 00:55:40.837
and I think it will
go on for many,

00:55:40.879 --> 00:55:43.298
many years in people's minds.

00:55:43.381 --> 00:55:52.849
[ Music ]

00:55:52.891 --> 00:56:02.901
[ Music ]

00:56:02.942 --> 00:56:06.946
[ Music ]

00:56:07.030 --> 00:56:19.042
[ Music ]

